  from "<scratch>/nospace/"     -> same fixture              -> EXIT 1, suite ran
Cause: scripts/no-vendor.mjs:446 `if (import.meta.url === \`file://\${process.argv[1]}\`) main();` — argv[1] is a raw
path, import.meta.url percent-encodes. Any checkout path with a space, '#', '?' or non-ASCII makes npm test exit 0
with the guard entirely absent and no output.

=================================== FINDINGS ===================================

B1 (BLOCKING) — the activation self-test, land 8's own proof, is the one mechanism in this opus that no
  assertion holds. A CLASS OF THREE, all measured above:
    (i)   scripts/no-vendor.mjs:390 — the `if (!selfTest(shadowDir, logPath))` fail-closed gate can be deleted
          outright and scripts/no-vendor.test.mjs stays green (M3).
    (ii)  scripts/no-vendor.mjs:191 — selfTest's own observation can be replaced by `const fired = true` and the
          suite stays green (M12). Behaviour 4 asserts only that selfTest returns true when the shim DOES fire.
    (iii) behaviour 11's second clause — "`.bisellium` is in the exclusion set `verify` computes" — has no
          assertion: removing ".bisellium" from verify.ts:155 leaves no-vendor.test.mjs green (M15), and no test
          in the repo asserts it (grep '\.bisellium' over every *.test.ts/*.test.mjs: branch.test.ts and
          check.test.ts PASS the string into their own helpers, they do not assert verify computes it).
  Searched: every exported function in scripts/no-vendor.mjs mutated individually, plus both shims (13 mutants),
  plus the cross-file clause (1). No fourth instance: bisdir preflight, lock acquire/refuse/release, shim
  existence + exec bit, all three static pins, adjudication, PATH order, exit-code passthrough, shim exit code
  and shim caller attribution each have a killing assertion.
  Why blocking and not advisory: the brief's own behaviour 4 states "a shim that silently fails to fire makes the
  runner exit before spending anything", and that sentence is the entire difference between this opus and rev 1's
  deleted fail-open. It is a claim with no assertion behind it — the class W-046's lesson names. The remedy is one
  row in behaviour 3's existing five-row table (a shim present and executable that writes nothing: assert non-zero
  + marker absent) plus one line in behaviour 4, and one assertion for (iii).

B2 (BLOCKING) — the runner's main guard fails OPEN on a percent-encoding checkout path: npm test exits 0 having
  run nothing and installed no guard (§8, measured both directions). This is the exact degraded mode land 8
  deleted, and worse than rev 1's, because rev 1's at least announced itself. The brief's own standard applies
  ("'unreachable today' is load-bearing luck", land 4) — the remedy is one line:
  `fileURLToPath(import.meta.url) === resolve(process.argv[1])`.
  ONE INSTANCE IN THIS OPUS. The idiom is house-wide — changelog.mjs:54, usage-from-workflow.mjs:187,
  backlog-page.mjs:276, sweep-traditio-stage.mjs:95 — but in those four a silent no-op is cosmetic; here it is
  the test gate. Searched: grep 'import.meta.url ===|argv\[1\]' over scripts/*.mjs and packages/cli/src/main.ts.
  The other four are a successor's, not this opus's.

